<!-- question: 169566 -->
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Complex Analysis - Liouville Application</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Suppose \(f(z)\) is an analytic function such that \(f(1/n) = \frac{1}{n+{@k@}}\) for all positive integers \(n\).</p>
<p>If such a function exists, determine the value of \(z\) where \(f(z)\) must have a singularity (pole).</p>
<p>\(z = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Let \(z = 1/n\). Then \(n = 1/z\).</p>
<p>Substituting this into the given relation:</p>
<p>\[ f(z) = \frac{1}{1/z + {@k@}} = \frac{z}{1 + {@k@}z} \]</p>
<p>By the Identity Theorem, this must be the form of \(f(z)\) everywhere.</p>
<p>This function has a singularity when the denominator is zero:</p>
<p>\[ 1 + {@k@}z = 0 \implies z = -\frac{1}{ {@k@} } \]</p>
<p>Since \(f(z)\) has a pole at \(z = -1/{@k@}\), it cannot be an entire function.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
